// THE MERGE-vs-REPLACE WITNESS. Two DIFFERENT fields of one Drop-carrying
// holder are written in one frame; the FIRST source dies at the inner scope's
// end, the second outlives it. The mechanism this pins is not "the field door
// deposits" — the two imported dropck ports
// this landing closed pin that — it is that
// the deposit is keyed PER FIELD PATH.
//
// If `dropck_borrow_sources_[root]` is written whole (the form this landed
// from), `w.b = &o` ERASES the record of `&inner` and this program compiles.
// MEASURED at the landing: rc 0 under the replacing form, rc 1 under per-path,
// with the probe firing twice in BOTH — the loss is silent, not a missed site.
//
// ⚠ ITS PAIR IS `bc_dropck_field_two_paths_swapped_fail`, WHICH IS THIS
// PROGRAM WITH THE TWO WRITES SWAPPED. Under the replacing form the swapped
// one REFUSES and this one ADMITS: same program, same fire count, opposite
// verdict, decided by write order alone. Only running both catches that; a
// single fixture here would have passed on the defective form 50% of the time.
package bc_dropck_field_two_paths_fail;
struct W<'a> { a: &'a i64, b: &'a i64 }
impl<'a> Drop for W<'a> { fn drop(self: &mut Self) { let v: i64 = *self.a; if v < 0i64 { return; } } }
fn main() -> i32 {
    let o: i64 = 5i64;
    let mut w: W = W { a: &o, b: &o };
    {
        let inner: i64 = 7i64;
        w.a = &inner;
        w.b = &o;
    }
    return 0i32;
}
